  "correctAnswer": "D",
  "explanation": "Choice D is correct. Setting $h(x)=0$ gives $x(x+3)(x-3)=0$. By the zero product property, this equation is satisfied when $x=0$, when $x+3=0$ (so $x=-3$), or when $x-3=0$ (so $x=3$). Therefore, there are $3$ distinct values of $x$ for which $h(x)=0$.\n\nWhy C is incorrect:\n\nChoice C is incorrect. This may result from treating $(x+3)(x-3)$ as yielding only one zero rather than recognizing it produces two distinct zeros at $x=-3$ and $x=3$.",
